# Feature: [ietf-yang-types]: Identifier Data Types

## Parent Epic
- [ ] #5 - [[ietf-yang-types]: Common YANG Data Types](https://github.com/gintatkinson/3dgs-037/blob/main/docs/epics/epic-01-ietf-yang-types.md) (Parent Epic for common YANG data types)

## Description
This feature specifies the set of identifier data types defined in the `ietf-yang-types` YANG module (`ietf-yang-types@2025-12-22.yang`, RFC 9911). These identifier types provide standardized string representations for hierarchical registration names, RFC 9562 Universally Unique Identifiers (UUIDs), and RFC 7950 YANG language identifiers.

The feature encompasses four canonical typedef specifications:

1. **`object-identifier`**:
   - Represents administratively assigned names in a registration-hierarchical-name tree as a sequence of dot-separated non-negative numerical sub-identifiers (e.g., `1.3.6.1.4.1`).
   - **Sub-identifier Numeric Range:** Each sub-identifier MUST NOT exceed $2^{32}-1$ (4,294,967,295).
   - **ASN.1 Standard Structural Restrictions:**
     - The first sub-identifier MUST be `0`, `1`, or `2`.
     - If the first sub-identifier is `0` or `1`, the second sub-identifier MUST be in the range `0` to `39` inclusive.
     - An object identifier MUST contain at least two sub-identifiers.
   - **Regex Pattern:** `(([0-1](\.[1-3]?[0-9]))|(2\.(0|([1-9][0-9]*))))(\.(0|([1-9][0-9]*)))*`
   - **SMIv2 Super-Set:** Unlike SMIv2 `OBJECT IDENTIFIER`, `object-identifier` allows more than 128 sub-identifiers.

2. **`object-identifier-128`**:
   - Derived from `object-identifier` but strictly restricted to at most 128 sub-identifiers (1 to 128 sub-identifiers).
   - **Regex Pattern:** `[0-9]*(\.[0-9]*){1,127}` (applied on top of `object-identifier`).
   - **SMIv2 Equivalence:** Semantically equivalent to the SMIv2 `OBJECT IDENTIFIER` type defined in RFC 2578.

3. **`uuid`**:
   - Represents a Universally Unique IDentifier formatted as a 36-character string in accordance with RFC 9562 (8-4-4-4-12 hex digits separated by hyphens).
   - **Canonical Form:** MUST use lowercase hexadecimal characters.
   - **Regex Pattern:** `[0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}`
   - **Example:** `f81d4fae-7dec-11d0-a765-00a0c91e6bf6`

4. **`yang-identifier`**:
   - Represents a valid YANG identifier string as defined by Section 14 of RFC 7950 (YANG 1.1).
   - **Length Restriction:** `1..max` (at least 1 character).
   - **Syntax Rules:** MUST start with an alphabetic character (`a-z`, `A-Z`) or an underscore (`_`), followed by any combination of alphabetic characters, digits (`0-9`), underscores (`_`), hyphens (`-`), or dots (`.`).
   - **Regex Pattern:** `[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9\-_.]*`
   - **YANG 1.1 Context:** Aligns with RFC 7950; the legacy YANG 1.0 (RFC 6020 / RFC 6991) restriction prohibiting leading `xml` prefixes is lifted in YANG 1.1 contexts.

## UML Class Diagram
```mermaid
classDiagram
    class IdentifierTypes {
        +String containerId "[1]"
    }
    class ObjectIdentifier {
        +String value "[1]"
        +Boolean validateAsn1Restrictions() "[1]"
        +Boolean validateSubIdentifierLimits() "[1]"
    }
    class ObjectIdentifier128 {
        +Integer maxSubIdentifiers "[1]"
        +Boolean validate128Limit() "[1]"
    }
    class Uuid {
        +String value "[1]"
        +Boolean validateRfc9562Format() "[1]"
        +Boolean isCanonicalLowercase() "[1]"
    }
    class YangIdentifier {
        +String value "[1]"
        +Integer minLength "[1]"
        +Boolean validateRfc7950Rules() "[1]"
    }
    IdentifierTypes "1" *-- "0..1" ObjectIdentifier : objectIdentifier
    IdentifierTypes "1" *-- "0..1" ObjectIdentifier128 : objectIdentifier128
    IdentifierTypes "1" *-- "0..1" Uuid : uuid
    IdentifierTypes "1" *-- "0..1" YangIdentifier : yangIdentifier
    ObjectIdentifier <|-- ObjectIdentifier128
```

## Interface Requirements

### 1. Payload Schema
```json
{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "title": "IdentifierTypesPayload",
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "objectIdentifier": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^(([0-1](\\.[1-3]?[0-9]))|(2\\.(0|([1-9][0-9]*))))(\\.(0|([1-9][0-9]*)))*$",
      "description": "ASN.1 OID string with sub-identifiers up to 2^32-1"
    },
    "objectIdentifier128": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^(([0-1](\\.[1-3]?[0-9]))|(2\\.(0|([1-9][0-9]*))))(\\.(0|([1-9][0-9]*)))*$",
      "maxLength": 1408,
      "description": "ASN.1 OID string restricted to at most 128 sub-identifiers"
    },
    "uuid": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^[0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}$",
      "description": "RFC 9562 UUID string in hex notation"
    },
    "yangIdentifier": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "pattern": "^[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9\\-_.]*$",
      "description": "YANG 1.1 identifier string per RFC 7950 Section 14"
    }
  },
  "required": ["objectIdentifier", "uuid", "yangIdentifier"]
}
```

### 2. Validation & Constraints
- **`object-identifier` Validation Rules:**
  - MUST contain at least two dot-separated numerical components.
  - First sub-identifier MUST be `0`, `1`, or `2`.
  - Second sub-identifier MUST be $\le 39$ if first sub-identifier is `0` or `1`.
  - Every sub-identifier value MUST be in the range $[0, 4294967295]$.
  - No leading zeroes allowed in sub-identifiers except for single digit `0`.
  - No whitespace permitted anywhere in the string.
- **`object-identifier-128` Validation Rules:**
  - Inherits all `object-identifier` validation rules.
  - Count of dot-separated sub-identifiers MUST NOT exceed 128.
- **`uuid` Validation Rules:**
  - String length MUST be exactly 36 characters (32 hex characters and 4 hyphens).
  - Format MUST match `8-4-4-4-12` pattern.
  - Canonical format SHOULD enforce lowercase hexadecimal characters (`0-9a-f`).
- **`yang-identifier` Validation Rules:**
  - Length MUST be at least 1 character (`1..max`).
  - First character MUST be in set `[a-zA-Z_]`.
  - Subsequent characters MUST be in set `[a-zA-Z0-9\-_.]`.

### 3. Logical Operations & Interface Messages
- **`validateIdentifier(type: String, value: String) -> ValidationResult`**:
  - Validates a given identifier string against the constraints of the specified typedef (`object-identifier`, `object-identifier-128`, `uuid`, or `yang-identifier`).
  - Returns `ValidationResult(isValid: Boolean, errorCode: String, message: String)`.
- **`normalizeUuid(value: String) -> String`**:
  - Converts a valid UUID string to its canonical lowercase representation.
- **`parseOidSubIdentifiers(value: String) -> List<Integer>`**:
  - Parses an OID string into a list of 32-bit unsigned sub-identifier integers. Throws `INVALID_OID_FORMAT` or `SUBIDENTIFIER_OVERFLOW` if validation fails.

### 4. Logical Exception States & Validation Failures
- **`INVALID_OID_FIRST_ARC`**:
  - Triggered when the first sub-identifier of an OID is $> 2$.
- **`INVALID_OID_SECOND_ARC`**:
  - Triggered when the first sub-identifier is `0` or `1` and the second sub-identifier is $> 39$.
- **`OID_SUBIDENTIFIER_OVERFLOW`**:
  - Triggered when a sub-identifier value exceeds $2^{32}-1$ (4,294,967,295).
- **`OID_TOO_FEW_SUBIDENTIFIERS`**:
  - Triggered when an OID contains fewer than 2 sub-identifiers (e.g. `"1"`).
- **`OID_128_LIMIT_EXCEEDED`**:
  - Triggered when an `object-identifier-128` contains more than 128 sub-identifiers.
- **`INVALID_UUID_FORMAT`**:
  - Triggered when a UUID string fails the `8-4-4-4-12` regex pattern.
- **`INVALID_YANG_IDENTIFIER_START`**:
  - Triggered when a `yang-identifier` starts with an invalid character (e.g. digit, hyphen, or dot).
- **`INVALID_YANG_IDENTIFIER_CHARACTER`**:
  - Triggered when a `yang-identifier` contains illegal characters (e.g. spaces, colons, or slashes).

## Given-When-Then Acceptance Criteria

### Scenario 1: Validating Standard Object Identifiers (OIDs)
- **Given** a data input stream expecting an `object-identifier` value
- **When** the payload receives valid OID strings `"1.3.6.1.4.1"`, `"0.39.100"`, or `"2.999.4294967295"`
- **Then** the validation engine SHALL accept the value as valid `object-identifier`
- **And** confirm that all sub-identifiers satisfy ASN.1 arc limits and 32-bit unsigned boundaries.

### Scenario 2: Rejecting Invalid OID First and Second Arc Combinations
- **Given** an OID validator for `object-identifier`
- **When** the input string is `"3.1.2"` (invalid first arc) or `"0.40.1"` (invalid second arc for root 0)
- **Then** the validation engine SHALL reject the input
- **And** raise error code `INVALID_OID_FIRST_ARC` or `INVALID_OID_SECOND_ARC` respectively.

### Scenario 3: Rejecting Sub-identifier Overflow
- **Given** an OID validator for `object-identifier`
- **When** the input string contains a sub-identifier exceeding $2^{32}-1$, such as `"1.3.4294967296"`
- **Then** the validation engine SHALL reject the input with `OID_SUBIDENTIFIER_OVERFLOW`.

### Scenario 4: Validating `object-identifier-128` Boundary Limits
- **Given** an `object-identifier-128` validator
- **When** an OID string with 128 sub-identifiers is evaluated
- **Then** the validation engine SHALL accept the value
- **When** an OID string with 129 sub-identifiers is evaluated
- **Then** the validation engine SHALL reject the input with error code `OID_128_LIMIT_EXCEEDED`.

### Scenario 5: Validating and Normalizing RFC 9562 UUID Strings
- **Given** a UUID validation function
- **When** the input is `"f81d4fae-7dec-11d0-a765-00a0c91e6bf6"` or uppercase `"F81D4FAE-7DEC-11D0-A765-00A0C91E6BF6"`
- **Then** the validation engine SHALL confirm it matches the RFC 9562 pattern
- **And** normalize the uppercase input to canonical lowercase `"f81d4fae-7dec-11d0-a765-00a0c91e6bf6"`.

### Scenario 6: Rejecting Malformed UUID Strings
- **Given** a UUID validation function
- **When** input strings `"f81d4fae-7dec-11d0-a765"` or `"f81d4fae-7dec-11d0-a765-00a0c91e6bf60"` are provided
- **Then** the validation engine SHALL reject the input with `INVALID_UUID_FORMAT`.

### Scenario 7: Validating RFC 7950 YANG Identifiers
- **Given** a `yang-identifier` validator compliant with RFC 7950
- **When** input strings `"interfaces"`, `"_bgp-peer.1"`, or `"xml-element"` are provided
- **Then** the validation engine SHALL accept the inputs as valid `yang-identifier` values.

### Scenario 8: Rejecting Invalid YANG Identifiers
- **Given** a `yang-identifier` validator
- **When** input strings `"123-node"` (starts with digit) or `"interface:eth0"` (contains colon) are provided
- **Then** the validation engine SHALL reject `"123-node"` with `INVALID_YANG_IDENTIFIER_START`
- **And** reject `"interface:eth0"` with `INVALID_YANG_IDENTIFIER_CHARACTER`.

## Specification Context (Verbatim)

From `schema/ietf-yang-types@2025-12-22.yang`:

```yang
  typedef object-identifier {
    type string {
      pattern '(([0-1](\.[1-3]?[0-9]))|(2\.(0|([1-9][0-9]*))))'
            + '(\.(0|([1-9][0-9]*)))*';
    }
    description
      "The object-identifier type represents administratively
       assigned names in a registration-hierarchical-name tree.

       Values of this type are denoted as a sequence of numerical
       non-negative sub-identifier values.  Each sub-identifier
       value MUST NOT exceed 2^32-1 (4294967295).  Sub-identifiers
       are separated by single dots and without any intermediate
       whitespace.

       The ASN.1 standard restricts the value space of the first
       sub-identifier to 0, 1, or 2.  Furthermore, the value space
       of the second sub-identifier is restricted to the range
       0 to 39 if the first sub-identifier is 0 or 1.  Finally,
       the ASN.1 standard requires that an object identifier
       has always at least two sub-identifiers.  The pattern
       captures these restrictions.

       Although the number of sub-identifiers is not limited,
       module designers should realize that there may be
       implementations that stick with the SMIv2 limit of 128
       sub-identifiers.

       This type is a superset of the SMIv2 OBJECT IDENTIFIER type
       since it is not restricted to 128 sub-identifiers.  Hence,
       this type SHOULD NOT be used to represent the SMIv2 OBJECT
       IDENTIFIER type; the object-identifier-128 type SHOULD be
       used instead.";
    reference
      "ISO 9834-1: Information technology -- Procedures for the
       operation of object identifier registration authorities --
       Part 1: General procedures and top arcs of the international
       object identifier tree";
  }

  typedef object-identifier-128 {
    type object-identifier {
      pattern '[0-9]*(\.[0-9]*){1,127}';
    }
    description
      "This type represents object-identifiers restricted to 128
       sub-identifiers.

       In the value set and its semantics, this type is equivalent
       to the OBJECT IDENTIFIER type of the SMIv2.";
    reference
      "RFC 2578: Structure of Management Information Version 2
                 (SMIv2)";
  }

  typedef uuid {
    type string {
      pattern '[0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-'
            + '[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}';
    }
    description
      "A Universally Unique IDentifier in the string representation
       defined in RFC 9562.  The canonical representation uses
       lowercase characters.

       The following is an example of a UUID in string
       representation:
       f81d4fae-7dec-11d0-a765-00a0c91e6bf6.
      ";
    reference
      "RFC 9562: Universally Unique IDentifiers (UUIDs)";
  }

  typedef yang-identifier {
    type string {
      length "1..max";
      pattern '[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9\-_.]*';
    }
    description
      "A YANG identifier string as defined by the 'identifier'
       rule in Section 14 of RFC 7950.  An identifier must
       start with an alphabetic character or an underscore
       followed by an arbitrary sequence of alphabetic or
       numeric characters, underscores, hyphens, or dots.

       This definition conforms to YANG 1.1 defined in RFC
       7950.  In RFC 6991, this definition excluded
       all identifiers starting with any possible combination
       of the lowercase or uppercase character sequence 'xml',
       as required by YANG 1 defined in RFC 6020.  If this type
       is used in a YANG 1 context, then this restriction still
       applies.";
    reference
      "RFC 7950: The YANG 1.1 Data Modeling Language
       RFC 6991: Common YANG Data Types
       RFC 6020: YANG - A Data Modeling Language for the
                 Network Configuration Protocol (NETCONF)";
  }
```

